{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018rNM1V7kkDqGQzLgotQxhk","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"type\": \"chapter\",\n  \"title\": null,\n  \"index\": 1,\n  \"start_position\": 6044,\n  \"end_position\": 90450,\n  \"word_count\": 15051,\n  \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 100.34,\n  \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n  \"children\": [],\n  \"characters_present\": [\n    \"Nick Carraway\",\n    \"Daisy Buchanan\",\n    \"Tom Buchanan\",\n    \"Jordan Baker\",\n    \"Myrtle Wilson\",\n    \"George Wilson\",\n    \"Catherine\",\n    \"Mr. McKee\",\n    \"Mrs. McKee\",\n    \"Owl Eyes\",\n    \"Gatsby\"\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest house in West Egg, renting it for eighty dollars a month beside the opulent mansions of Daisy Buchanan and the enigmatic Gatsby. He drives across the bay to East Egg for dinner at Daisy and Tom Buchanan\\u2019s Georgian Colonial, where Daisy\\u2019s ethereal charm, Tom\\u2019s aggressive dominance, and Jordan Baker\\u2019s silent presence establish an undercurrent of unease, culminating in Daisy accusing Tom of bruising her knuckle. That evening on the rosy porch, Tom delivers a racist monologue citing The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy, after a phone call, calls Nick 'an absolute rose' and later confesses she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'\\u2014a moment undercut by her smirk. Tom then takes Nick by train to the valley of ashes, introducing him to his mistress, Myrtle Wilson, at her husband George\\u2019s garage; they travel to New York by train, where Myrtle buys a $10 Airedale puppy and they ride in a lavender taxi to her apartment on 158th Street. Inside the overcrowded apartment, Myrtle, Catherine, and the McKees arrive, and after drinking whisky, Myrtle recounts her affair with Tom and Catherine falsely claims Daisy is Catholic to prevent divorce. The party descends into chaos when Tom breaks Myrtle\\u2019s nose after she shouts Daisy\\u2019s name, prompting a bloody, drunken scramble; Nick exits with Mr. McKee in the elevator, only to find himself later standing beside Gatsby\\u2019s bed, hearing him murmur, before waking alone at dawn in Pennsylvania Station. Meanwhile, at a late-night party at Gatsby\\u2019s mansion, Nick and Jordan search for the host, discover the drunken Owl Eyes verifying Gatsby\\u2019s real books, and meet Gatsby himself\\u2014struck by his rare smile. Gatsby speaks privately with Jordan, leaving her cryptic, as the party collapses into drunken fights and marital altercations. The night ends with Gatsby being called away by a Philadelphia phone call. The next morning, Nick witnesses a car crash outside Gatsby\\u2019s gate: a new coup\\u00e9 has lost a wheel, and two men emerge\\u2014one bewildered Owl Eyes claiming he wasn\\u2019t driving, the other dazed and asking if they ran out of gas\\u2014while Gatsby stands alone on the porch under a faint moon. In midsummer, Nick reflects on his daily routine as a clerk in New York, walking home past the city\\u2019s nightlife, reconnecting with Jordan, whom he discovers is dishonest after she lies about damaging a borrowed car and he recalls her scandalous golf tournament cheating; their conversation about driving reveals her careless attitude, and Nick concludes with his claim of being one of the few honest people he knows.\"\n}\n\n---\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"chapter\",\n  \"title\": \"IV\",\n  \"index\": 2,\n  \"start_position\": 90450,\n  \"end_position\": 94853,\n  \"word_count\": 763,\n  \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 5.086666666666667,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"children\": [],\n  \"characters_present\": [\n    \"Chester Beckers\",\n    \"Leeches\",\n    \"Bunsen\",\n    \"Doctor Webster Civet\",\n    \"Hornbeams\",\n    \"Willie Voltaires\",\n    \"Blackbuck clan\",\n    \"Ismays\",\n    \"Chrysties\",\n    \"Edgar Beaver\",\n    \"Clarence Endive\",\n    \"Cheadles\",\n    \"O. R. P. Schraeders\",\n    \"Stonewall Jackson Abrams\",\n    \"Fishguards\",\n    \"Ripley Snell\",\n    \"Dancies\",\n    \"S. B. Whitebait\",\n    \"Maurice A. Flink\",\n    \"Hammerheads\",\n    \"Beluga\",\n    \"Beluga\\u2019s girls\",\n    \"Poles\",\n    \"Mulreadys\",\n    \"Cecil Roebuck\",\n    \"Cecil Schoen\",\n    \"Gulick\",\n    \"Newton Orchid\",\n    \"Eckhaust\",\n    \"Clyde Cohen\",\n    \"Don S. Schwartz\",\n    \"Arthur McCarty\",\n    \"Catlips\",\n    \"Bembergs\",\n    \"G. Earl Muldoon\",\n    \"Da Fontano\",\n    \"Ed Legros\",\n    \"James B. ('Rot-Gut') Ferret\",\n    \"De Jongs\",\n    \"Ernest Lilly\",\n    \"Klipspringer\",\n    \"Gus Waize\",\n    \"Horace O\\u2019Donavan\",\n    \"Lester Myer\",\n    \"George Duckweed\",\n    \"Francis Bull\",\n    \"Chromes\",\n    \"Backhyssons\",\n    \"Dennickers\",\n    \"Russel Betty\",\n    \"Corrigans\",\n    \"Kellehers\",\n    \"Dewars\",\n    \"Scullys\",\n    \"S. W. Belcher\",\n    \"Smirkes\",\n    \"young Quinns\",\n    \"Henry L. Palmetto\",\n    \"Benny McClenahan\",\n    \"Faustina O\\u2019Brien\",\n    \"Baedeker girls\",\n    \"young Brewer\",\n    \"Mr. Albrucksburger\",\n    \"Miss Haag\",\n    \"Ardita Fitz-Peters\",\n    \"Mr. P. Jewett\",\n    \"Miss Claudia Hip\",\n    \"Duke\"\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"On a Sunday morning as church bells ring along the shore, Gatsby\\u2019s mansion becomes the center of a lavish, chaotic party attended by a sprawling array of guests from East Egg, West Egg, and New York. The narrator recalls names from a disintegrating timetable dated July 5th, 1922, listing aristocrats, movie producers, gamblers, and socialites\\u2014many arriving by automobile\\u2014who accept Gatsby\\u2019s hospitality while knowing nothing about him. Guests include the Chester Beckers, the Blackbucks, the Snells (who was run over by a car after drunkenness), Klipspringer (known as 'the boarder'), and a rotating group of identical young women accompanying Benny McClenahan.